All about image resources in recovery images
============================================

The code in "messages.sh" depends on a collection of PNG files
stored in /etc/screens on the initramfs root file system.  The
images are generated by the "make_images" script.


Contents of /etc/screens, and how it's generated
================================================

screens/background.png
  Background image displayed first of all before any other image or
  message.  For now, it's a solid color background.  Message image
  files should use the same solid color for their background.

screens/<locale>/<msgname>.png
  These are the localized individual message files displayed during
  recovery.  <locale> is a standard locale name (e.g. "en", "fr",
  "zh_CN", etc.).  <msgname> is the name used in "messages.sh".

  Localized message texts are stored under the "localized_text"
  source subdirectory; for each "localized_text/<locale>/<msgname>.txt"
  file, a corresponding ".png" file is created under "screens".

screens/progress_box.png
screens/progress_increment.png
  Images used for the progress bar in `progress_bar`.  The
  "progress_box" displays the progress bar at 0% completion.
  The "progress_increment" contains a column of pixels
  corresponding to a 1% increment.

screens/moving_box_??.png
  While we're installing the recovery image, we present an animation
  of a box that moves back and forth across the screen.  These files
  represent the individual frames of that animation.  Their generation
  is described in detail in comments in "make_image".


Adding a new locale
===================

All available locales are currently stored under localized_text;
however, not all locales are included in the final output.  Each
Chrome computer is manufactured to be used in a specific locale;
the 'screens' locale content is only generated for the locales to
which units may actually be shipped.

The list of supported locales is stored in a global setting at the
top of 'make_images'.  A new locale can be added by updating that
setting.  After making the change, you should go through the steps
below under "How to generate content."

Note that you should not accept on faith that simply updating
the list of supported locales is enough to produce a good set of
images.  A number of things may go wrong:
  * The text layout may not wrap lines properly, causing text to be
    truncated or otherwise mis-formatted.
  * Scripts that display right-to-left are untested, and may not
    render properly.  Most notable in this class are 'ar' (Arabic)
    and 'iw' (Hebrew).  Give these special attention when/if they're
    added.
  * Some fonts may be missing or incomplete.
  * The translators didn't have access to a Chromebook to observe
    the messages in context, and may have mis-translated some
    messages.
  * Translators are human, and sometimes make mistakes.

When adding a new locale, you should at minimum visually inspect the
images to confirm that it looks like the original text.  If you can,
it would be wise to find someone familiar with the target language
to confirm there are no glaring errors.

How to generate content
=======================

The work is done by the "make_images" script.  The script takes no
arguments.  It reads the contents under "localized_text" to create
the localized message screens, and then creates common content under
"screens".  The script regenerates the entire "screens" directory
from scratch every time it runs.

The workflow for creating the images goes something like this:
 1) Edit content under localized_text, and/or install translated
    content there.
 2) Outside the chroot, run "./make_images".
 3) Test that the content changes look correct.  This may include
    working with the translator and/or a UX designer.
 4) Repeat steps 1-3 until it's right.
 5) "git commit" your changes to "localized_text" and "screens",
    upload them, get them reviewed, and submit.  (This could, of
    course, entail repeating steps 1-4).
